<h1>Battery prices &amp; material costs</h1>
<p>Price is a function of chemistry, capacity, certification and the material curve underneath. Use these pages to separate a real price change from a quote that has simply expired.</p>
<h2>What moves battery prices</h2>
<p>Four inputs explain most of a battery quotation: the material curve (lead, lithium, nickel, graphite), the chemistry and capacity, the certification and documentation package, and the freight route. A price change without a move in those inputs is usually a commercial decision or a quote that has expired, not a market signal.</p>
<h2>How to read a quote</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Validity.</strong> A price without a validity date is an invitation to renegotiate, not a quote.</li>
<li><strong>Incoterm.</strong> EXW, FOB, CIF and DDP include different costs; compare the same term.</li>
<li><strong>Volume tier.</strong> The unit price at 10 units is not the price at 1,000.</li>
<li><strong>Documentation.</strong> COA, MSDS, UN38.3 and certification add cost; a cheaper quote may exclude them.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Price versus cost</h2>
<p>The lowest purchase price can be the highest cost over life if cycle life, efficiency or replacement intervals are worse. Use the total-cost-of-ownership approach for cycling applications, and the purchase price for low-cycle standby where the cycle-life advantage is never used.</p>

